'''adelantadillo''' (ä-dā-län-tä-dēl′yō), ''n.'' [Sp., dim. of ''adelantado'', advanced, early, applied to fruit or plants: see ''adelantado''.] A Spanish red wine made of the earliest ripe grapes.

'''adelantado''' (ä″dā-län-tä′dō), ''n.'' [Sp.; lit., advanced, forward; as applied to fruit or plants, early; pp. of ''adelantar'', advance, grow, anticipate, < ''adelante'', adv., forward, onward, < ''ad-'', ''á'' (< L. ''ad''), to, + ''el'', the (< L. ''ille'', that), + ''ante'' (< L. ''ante''), before.] The title formerly given in Spain to the governor of a province.
{{smaller block|Invincible ''adelantado'' over the army of pimpled . . . faces.
{{right|''Massinger'', Virgin-Martyr, ii. 1.}}
The marquess had a secret conference with Don Pedro Enriquez, ''Adelantado'' of Andalusia. {{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 29.}}}}

'''Adelarthrosomata''' (ad-ẹ̄-lär-thrō-sō′ma-tä), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < Gr. ἄδηλος, not manifest (< ἀ- priv., not, + δῆλος, manifest), + ἄρθρον, joint, + σῶμα, pl. σώματα, body.] In Westwood’s system of classification, an order of arachnids which respire by tracheæ. {{smaller|It consists of the false scorpions and harvestmen, or the families ''Solpugidæ'', ''Cheliferidæ'', and ''Phalangiidæ'': distinguished from the ''Monomerosomata''. With the view of adapting Leach’s system to that of Latreille, Westwood adopted Latreille’s three sections of ''Arachnida'', namely, ''Pulmonaria'', ''Trachearia'', and ''Aporobranchia'', dividing the first of these sections into the orders ''Dimerosomata'' and ''Polymerosomata'', the second section into the orders ''Adelarthrosomata'' and ''Monomero-''}}
